  "image_name": "3117f9daa1622c85e4fc048f0388ae48.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how an enhanced geothermal system is built and operated. It begins with drilling a deep injection well that curves from vertical to horizontal into hot rock. The next stage shows “multistage stimulation,” where fluid is pumped down the well to crack the surrounding rock and create a network of fractures around the horizontal section. A second, nearby well is then drilled as a production well and also reaches the fractured hot zone, forming a connected subsurface heat-exchange region.\n\nIn the final operating stage, cool water is injected downward through one well, circulates through the stimulated fracture network in the hot rock, heats up, and returns to the surface through the production well. At the surface, the hot fluid feeds a geothermal power plant, which is shown connected to transmission lines, indicating electricity generation.\n\nThe bottom panel gives a closer technical view of the completion method used to stimulate the well: a wireline carries perforating guns and a setting tool into the casing, a composite plug isolates sections, and cluster perforations are created in stages along the horizontal well. Overall, the image explains the stepwise development of geothermal energy from drilling to power production.",
